From: TrailCurrent
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: btusb: Use the 16-bit SCO alt setting when the
 voice setting is unset

The AIC8800D80 reports a voice setting of 0x0000 (8-bit samples). btusb
sizes CVSD isochronous transfers from that global value, so it picks alt 1,
while the connection itself, set up through an SCO socket, uses the default
BT_VOICE_CVSD_16BIT (0x0060). Half of each call's audio is lost and the
kernel logs "corrupted SCO packet"; calls sound choppy and pulsing.

Treat a voice setting of 0 as the 16-bit default when choosing the alt
setting.

Upstream-Status: Pending

---
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c
@@ -2374,7 +2374,13 @@
 		}
 
 		if (data->air_mode == HCI_NOTIFY_ENABLE_SCO_CVSD) {
-			if (hdev->voice_setting & 0x0020) {
+			/* A voice setting of 0 (8-bit samples) is what some
+			 * controllers report when nothing has set one. SCO
+			 * sockets default to 16-bit CVSD, so the connection
+			 * carries 16-bit samples, and sizing the transfers for
+			 * 8 bits loses half the audio and corrupts the rest.
+			 */
+			if ((hdev->voice_setting & 0x0020) || !hdev->voice_setting) {
 				static const int alts[3] = { 2, 4, 5 };
 				unsigned int sco_idx;
 
